  {
   "id": "p2-7",
   "name": "Density: definition, units and measurement"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Density is the mass of one unit of volume of a substance. Density = mass ÷ volume (ρ = m/V).\nUnits: g/cm³ (grams per cubic centimetre) or kg/m³. 1 g/cm³ = 1000 kg/m³. Water: 1 g/cm³.\nMeasuring density:\n• mass with a balance;\n• volume of a regular solid: length × width × height;\n• volume of an irregular solid (a stone): by displacement. Read the water in a measuring cylinder, put the stone in, read again: the volume of the stone is the difference.\nAn object less dense than water floats; a denser one sinks. Iron (7.9 g/cm³) sinks; wood (about 0.6 g/cm³) floats.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "m": 60,
   "a": 50,
   "b": 70
  },
  "problem": "A stone has a mass of {m} g. Water in a measuring cylinder rises from {a} cm³ to {b} cm³ when the stone is put in. Find its density.",
  "steps": [
   "Volume of the stone = {b} − {a} = {b - a} cm³.",
   "Density = {m} ÷ {b - a} = {density(m, b - a)} g/cm³."
  ],
  "answer": "{density(m, b - a)} g/cm³: it sinks in water.",
  "checks": [
   "density(m, b - a) == 3"
  ],
  "figure": "displacement(a, b)"
 },
